# EVA — ESP32 Interactive Robotic Head

EVA is an ESP32-based interactive robotic head developed as an embedded systems project. The system combines **sensor interfacing, real-time firmware architecture, signal processing, actuator control, event-driven behaviour, and PC-based visualization**.

The firmware is designed around a **Finite-State Machine (FSM), interrupt-driven sensing, median filtering, event handling, and cooperative non-blocking scheduling**.

---

## Engineering Highlights

- Finite-State Machine (FSM) for behaviour management
- Event-driven architecture using a fixed-size ring buffer
- Cooperative non-blocking scheduler
- Interrupt-driven ultrasonic time-of-flight measurement
- Five-sample median filtering
- Sensor validation and fault detection
- Threshold-based detection with confirmation logic
- I²C communication with SSD1306 OLED
- PWM-based servo control
- GPIO-based sensor and actuator interfacing
- UART/serial telemetry
- PC-based radar visualization using Processing
- Hardware–firmware integration and validation

---

## System Overview

EVA uses an ESP32 as the central controller. Sensor inputs are processed by the firmware and converted into system events and behavioural responses.

```text
                         ┌─────────────────────┐
                         │        ESP32        │
                         │  Central Controller │
                         └──────────┬──────────┘
                                    │
              ┌─────────────────────┼─────────────────────┐
              │                     │                     │
              ▼                     ▼                     ▼
          HC-SR04                TTP223               SSD1306
      Distance Sensing         Touch Input           OLED / I²C
              │                     │                     │
              └─────────────────────┼─────────────────────┘
                                    │
                                    ▼
                         ┌─────────────────────┐
                         │  Firmware Processing │
                         │                     │
                         │  • Validation       │
                         │  • Filtering        │
                         │  • Detection        │
                         │  • FSM              │
                         │  • Event Handling   │
                         │  • Scheduler        │
                         └──────────┬──────────┘
                                    │
                         ┌──────────┴──────────┐
                         ▼                     ▼
                       Servo                 LEDs
                  Head Movement        Distance Indication
                         │
                         ▼
                  Serial Telemetry
                         │
                         ▼
                 Processing Radar
Core Firmware Architecture

The EVA firmware uses a cooperative time-based scheduler instead of a blocking sequential loop.

Different tasks are executed according to their required update periods.

Task	Period
Touch	20 ms
Servo	20 ms
OLED	40 ms
Sensor	60 ms
Radar	60 ms
Telemetry	1 s

The main loop primarily calls the scheduler update function, allowing multiple system activities to operate without long blocking delays.

Ultrasonic Distance Measurement

The HC-SR04 is used for distance measurement.

The ESP32 generates a 10 µs trigger pulse and measures the echo pulse width using interrupt-based edge detection.

TRIG → GPIO 5
ECHO → GPIO 23

The rising edge records the start time and the falling edge determines the echo duration.

The measured distance is calculated from the echo pulse duration.

Valid measurements are restricted to:

3 cm ≤ distance ≤ 200 cm

A timeout prevents the system from remaining blocked when a valid echo is not received.

Signal Validation and Median Filtering

Ultrasonic measurements may occasionally contain invalid or abnormal readings.

EVA uses:

Valid range: 3–200 cm
Five-sample median filter
Consecutive bad-reading detection

Recent valid measurements are sorted and the middle value is selected as the median.

This reduces the effect of isolated abnormal measurements and provides more stable distance data for the detection logic.

HC-SR04 Logic-Level Interface

The HC-SR04 operates at 5 V while the ESP32 uses 3.3 V logic.

The ECHO signal is therefore connected to GPIO 23 through a resistor divider.

HC-SR04 ECHO
      │
    1 kΩ
      │
      ├────── GPIO 23
      │
    2 kΩ
      │
     GND

The divider produces approximately 3.3 V at the ESP32 input.

Person Detection

EVA does not declare a person detected from a single ultrasonic measurement.

In Normal Mode, a filtered distance within the 3–60 cm range is treated as a close-object condition.

Two consecutive close readings are required to confirm detection.

When the detected object moves away, exit confirmation and timing conditions are used before declaring that the person has left.

Distance Reading
       ↓
     Valid?
    ↙      ↘
   No       Yes
   ↓         ↓
 Fault   Median Filter
             ↓
          ≤ 60 cm?
          ↙     ↘
        Yes      No
         ↓        ↓
 Confirmation   Exit Logic
         ↓
   Person Detected
Distance Indicator

Five LEDs provide distance-based visual feedback.

LED	Threshold
LED1	≤100 cm
LED2	≤80 cm
LED3	≤60 cm
LED4	≤40 cm
LED5	≤20 cm

Each LED uses a 220 Ω current-limiting resistor.

Touch Interface

The TTP223 provides touch-based interaction through GPIO 4.

Debounce handling prevents false or repeated touch events.

Short touch → Touch event / LOVE behaviour
Long press → Operating mode change
OLED Interface

EVA uses an SSD1306 128×64 OLED through I²C.

Connection	ESP32
SDA	GPIO 21
SCL	GPIO 22
VCC	3.3 V
GND	GND
I²C Address	0x3C

The OLED provides visual feedback through:

Sleepy expression
Happy expression
Love expression
Observation information
Detection information
Servo Control

The SG90 servo provides physical movement of the robotic head.

Servo Signal → GPIO 19
Frequency    → 50 Hz

The firmware uses pulse-width control and incremental position updates.

Behaviour	Angular Range
Normal Mode	30°–150°
Observation Mode	0°–180°
Love Expression	84°–96°

The motion engine updates the servo position incrementally so that servo movement does not block other system tasks.

Finite-State Behaviour

EVA uses state-based behaviour to determine its current response.

The main behavioural states are:

SLEEPY
HAPPY
LOVE
OBSERVATION
Normal Mode
No person detected → SLEEPY

Person detected     → HAPPY

Touch detected      → LOVE

The state-based architecture separates system condition from the corresponding physical and visual response.

Event-Driven Architecture

Instead of directly executing every response inside sensor-reading functions, EVA uses an event-driven approach.

Important events include:

Person entered
Person left
Touch pressed
Mode changed

Events are stored in a fixed-size ring buffer and processed by the event handler.

This separates:

Event Detection
      ↓
Event Queue
      ↓
Event Processing
      ↓
Behaviour Response
Observation Mode

Observation Mode extends EVA from interactive behaviour to environmental scanning.

The servo continuously scans:

0° → 180°

The observation detection zone is:

20 cm ≤ distance ≤ 100 cm

The system records detection episodes and maintains an estimated current occupancy.

A single HC-SR04 cannot identify individual people or distinguish multiple unique people simultaneously. Therefore, Observation Mode represents detection episodes and estimated occupancy rather than unique human identities.

Radar Data Pipeline

During Observation Mode, EVA sends radar information through serial communication.

Data format:

RADAR,angle,distance,detected

Example:

RADAR,90,45.2,1

Data flow:

HC-SR04
   ↓
Distance Measurement
   ↓
ESP32
   ↓
Servo Angle + Distance
   ↓
Serial Communication
   ↓
Processing
   ↓
Radar Visualization
Radar Visualization

A Processing application receives the serial data and maps the measured angle and distance onto a radar display.

Angular Range    → 0°–180°
Distance Range   → 0–100 cm

The visualization maintains distance and detection history for each scan angle.

GPIO Mapping
Device	Function	ESP32 GPIO
OLED	SDA	21
OLED	SCL	22
HC-SR04	TRIG	5
HC-SR04	ECHO	23
TTP223	OUT	4
SG90 Servo	Signal	19
LED1	Distance indication	13
LED2	Distance indication	12
LED3	Distance indication	14
LED4	Distance indication	26
LED5	Distance indication	25
Power and Logic Architecture

The ESP32 development board provides the required power rails.

ESP32 3.3 V
    ├── OLED
    └── TTP223

ESP32 5 V / VIN
    ├── HC-SR04
    └── SG90 Servo

ESP32 GND
    └── Common Ground

The HC-SR04 ECHO line uses a 1 kΩ / 2 kΩ resistor divider before connection to GPIO 23.

Hardware
